"[2511.17473v1] Masked-and-Reordered Self-Supervision for Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards (https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17473v1)\nciteturn1947view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17473v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 21 Nov 2025]\nL9: # Title:Masked-and-Reordered Self-Supervision for Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Zhen Wang , cite6†Zhifeng Gao , cite7†Guolin Ke L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Masked-and-Reordered Self-Supervision for Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards, by Zhen Wang and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite8†View PDF cite9†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Test-time scaling has been shown to substantially improve large language models' (LLMs) mathematical reasoning. However, for a large portion of mathematical corpora, especially theorem proving, RLVR's scalability is limited: intermediate reasoning is crucial, while final answers are difficult to directly and reliably verify. Meanwhile, token-level SFT often degenerates into rote memorization rather than inducing longer chains of thought.\nL17: Inspired by BERT's self-supervised tasks, we propose MR-RLVR (Masked-and-Reordered RLVR), which constructs process-level self-supervised rewards via \"masked-then-fill\" and \"step reordering\" to extract learnable signals from intermediate reasoning. Our training pipeline comprises two stages: we first perform self-supervised training on sampled mathematical calculation and proof data; we then conduct RLVR fine-tuning on mathematical calculation datasets where only outcomes are verifiable.\nL18: We implement MR-RLVR on Qwen2.5-3B and DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-1.5B, and evaluate on AIME24, AIME25, AMC23, and MATH500. Under a fixed sampling and decoding budget, MR-RLVR achieves average relative gains over the original RLVR of +9.86% Pass@1, +5.27% Pass@5, and +4.00% Pass@8. These results indicate that incorporating process-aware self-supervised signals can effectively enhance RLVR's scalability and performance in only outcome-verifiable settings.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite10†arXiv:2511.17473 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite11†arXiv:2511.17473v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite12†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2511.17473†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Zhen Wang [cite13†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Fri, 21 Nov 2025 18:23:04 UTC (6,523 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Masked-and-Reordered Self-Supervision for Reinforcement Learning from Verifiable Rewards, by Zhen Wang and 2 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite8†View PDF L35:   * cite9†HTML (experimental) L36:   * cite14†TeX Source L37: \nL38: cite15†view license L39: ### Current browse context:\nL40: \nL41: cs.CL\nL42: \nL43: cite16†< prev |   cite17†next > L44: \nL45: cite18†new | cite19†recent | cite20†2025-11 L46: \nL47: Change to browse by:\nL48: \nL49: cite21†cs L50: cite22†cs.AI L51: cite23†cs.LG L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite24†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite25†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite26†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L58: \nL59: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL60: \nL61: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL62: \nL63: [Button: ×]\nL64: \nL65: loading...\nL66: \nL67: Data provided by:\nL68: \nL69: 